  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how anti-tumor T cell immunity is initiated in lymphoid tissue and then regulated within the tumor microenvironment. On the left, an antigen-presenting cell takes up tumor antigens and activates T cells through two linked signals: antigen recognition via MHC–TCR interactions and co-stimulation through CD28 binding B7 molecules. A competing inhibitory pathway, CTLA4, dampens CD4+ T cell activation. Activated CD4+ T cells then provide helper cytokines that support CD8+ T cell priming and expansion.\n\nOn the right, these activated T cells move into the tumor microenvironment. CD4+ cells appear to differentiate into TH1 cells that produce IFNγ, which stimulates macrophages and also supports anti-tumor activity. CD8+ T cells engage tumor cells directly, but their function is restrained by immune checkpoint signaling through PD1 on T cells and PDL1 on surrounding cells, including tumor-associated cells and macrophages. Regulatory T cells add another layer of suppression, inhibiting the CD8+ response.\n\nOverall, the image contrasts activating signals that generate effective anti-tumor immunity with inhibitory checkpoint and suppressive mechanisms in the tumor site that limit CD8+ T cell killing.",
